BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:NC Cultural Resources
CATEGORIES:Education Programs,Lectures & Talks
DESCRIPTION:Join a Ranger to hike 3 miles round trip to the site of a cemet
 ery of enslaved people. We will discuss laws and statistics related to the 
 practice of slavery in NC. This hike does feature discussions about racial 
 violence and content that is more suited to a mature audience. Please atten
 d with a respectful attitude and be prepared for a significant creek crossi
 ng and some short sections of off-trail hiking.\n\nPlease be sure to dress 
 appropriately for the weather\, wear hiking shoes\, and bring water!\n\nSpa
 ces are limited\, and registration is required. To sign up\, call (984) 250
 -1370 or email eno.river@ncparks.gov. Include the number of guests in your 
 party and a phone number where you can be reached if the event is changed o
 r canceled.
DTEND:20260521T220000Z
DTSTAMP:20260508T035723Z
DTSTART:20260521T200000Z
GEO:36.078519;-79.005129
LOCATION:Eno River State Park - Fews Ford Access\, Loop Parking Lot (End of
  the Road)
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:Untitled Hike
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_52702226064645
URL:https://events.dncr.nc.gov/event/untitled-hike-827
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:NC Cultural Resources
CATEGORIES:Education Programs
DESCRIPTION:Author Talk: Marcia Phillips — The Regulator Movement in North 
 Carolina\nFree Admission\n\nJoin historian\, author\, and preservationist M
 arcia Phillips for an engaging exploration of North Carolina’s overlooked e
 arly freedom fighters—the Regulators. In this compelling talk\, Phillips un
 covers how backcountry settlers in the 1760s and early 1770s rose up agains
 t corrupt colonial leadership\, unjust taxation\, and an unequal legal syst
 em—years before the outbreak of the American Revolution.\n\nWhile much of t
 he Revolution’s story centers on cities like Boston and Philadelphia\, this
  program highlights the critical role of the southern colonies and the Caro
 linian frontier. Through vivid storytelling and historical insight\, Philli
 ps connects the Regulator movement to the broader struggle for independence
 \, showing how it helped shape revolutionary thought\, deepen divisions bet
 ween Loyalists and Patriots\, and influence the Southern Campaign of the Re
 volutionary War.\n\nThis program offers a deeper understanding of how the f
 ight for American liberty began not only in the North\, but in the hills an
 d backcountry of pre-Revolutionary North Carolina.\n\nA book sale and signi
 ng will follow the presentation. Refreshments will be provided.\n\nDate & T
 ime: Thursday\, May 21\, 6:00 PM\nLocation: Genealogy & Local History Room\
 nLexington Public Library\n612 S. Main Street\, Lexington\, NC 27292\n\nCo-
 hosted by the Davidson County Historical Museum and the Lexington Public Li
 brary Genealogy & Local History Room.
DTEND:20260521T230000Z
DTSTAMP:20260508T035723Z
DTSTART:20260521T220000Z
GEO:35.819216;-80.260352
LOCATION:Lexington Public Library\, Genealogy & Local History Room
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:Author Talk: Marcia Phillips - The Regulator Movement in North Caro
 lina
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_52816255483582
URL:https://events.dncr.nc.gov/event/author-talk-marcia-phillips-the-regula
 tor-movement-in-north-carolina
END:VEVENT
