BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:NC Cultural Resources
CATEGORIES:Education Programs,Lectures & Talks
DESCRIPTION:Have you heard of the Tory War? From 1780-81\, Cumberland Count
 y and the surrounding region fell into a civil war between Tories and Patri
 ots during the American Revolution. Learn the stories of courage and horror
  that took place in this formative period in the region's history! \n\nThis
  project was produced with assistance from the America 250 North Carolina i
 nitiative\, administered by the North Carolina Department of Natural and Cu
 ltural Resources. Any opinions\, findings and conclusions or recommendation
 s expressed in this material are those of the author(s) and do not necessar
 ily reflect the views of the State of North Carolina.
DTEND:20260718T180000Z
DTSTAMP:20260508T035733Z
DTSTART:20260718T170000Z
GEO:35.055443;-78.881263
LOCATION:Cumberland County Public Library\, Local & State History Room
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:The Tory War
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_52434796507001
URL:https://events.dncr.nc.gov/event/the-tory-war-9501
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:NC Cultural Resources
CATEGORIES:Exhibits & Tours,Exhibitions,Education Programs,Lectures & Talks
DESCRIPTION:Join us to celebrate the opening of our next exhibition\, ᏔᎷᏥ Ꮥ
 ᎬᏍᎬ ᎣᎩᎲᎢ Weaving our Heritage: Cherokee Baskets. The opening reception will
  begin in the BRAHM community meeting room for refreshments and a conversat
 ion with exhibition curator\, Anna Fareillo. The framing conversation will 
 discuss the traditions and innovations of Cherokee baskets. As well as how 
 the artist's work is a reflection of economic\, environmental\, and cultura
 l influences.\n\nThis event is made possible through a grant from America 2
 50 NC\, an initiative by the North Carolina Department of Natural and Cultu
 ral Resources.\n\nAbout the Exhibition:\n\nBasket weaving is one of the old
 est forms of material culture\, dating back thousands of years. It’s practi
 ce among indigenous people sustainably uses—and honors—the natural material
 s that make up their environment. Cherokee baskets are made of rivercane\, 
 white oak\, maple\, or honeysuckle and are dyed with local plant materials.
  Today’s Eastern Band of Cherokee people live in western North Carolina and
  continue to maintain their time-honored traditions\, while expanding their
  artistic voice to include contemporary adaptations.\n\nArtists on Exhibiti
 on:\nGabe Crowe\, Louise Goings\, Lauren Goings\, Shan Goshorn\, Ramona Los
 sie\,  Lucille Lossiah\, Betty Maney\, Emma Taylor\, Mary Thompson\, Sarah 
 Thompson\, Dewi Thompson\, Geraldine Walkingstick\, Agnes Welch\, Carol Wel
 ch\, Eva Wolfe (pictured).\n\nAbout the Curator:\nAnna Fariello\, an indepe
 ndent curator with Curatorial InSight\, has organized over forty exhibition
 s on art and cultural history. She has written numerous articles and four b
 ooks on Cherokee arts and culture.
DTEND:20260718T190000Z
DTSTAMP:20260508T035733Z
DTSTART:20260718T180000Z
GEO:36.130278;-81.679307
LOCATION:Blowing Rock Art & History Museum
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:Weaving our Heritage: Cherokee Baskets Exhibition Opening Reception
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_52576193440631
URL:https://events.dncr.nc.gov/event/weaving-our-heritage-cherokee-baskets-
 exhibition-opening-reception
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:NC Cultural Resources
CATEGORIES:Exhibits & Tours,Guided Tours
DESCRIPTION:Step into Tryon Palace and explore Diana Gabaldon’s award-winni
 ng Outlander series. Learn about the lives of the fictional Jamie and Clair
 e Fraser\, their family and friends\, and interactions with historical figu
 res and events in the tumultuous period of the American Revolution.\n\nTour
  covers storylines from Outlander books 4 – 8 and the Starz Outlander seaso
 ns 4 – 8. (For those not current on the books or show\, there will be spoil
 ers for these books and seasons.)\n\nTour begins at 301 George Street\, acr
 oss from the Tryon Palace Main Gates. Ticket holders should check in at the
  Waystation ticketing desk no later than 3:30 p.m.
DTEND:20260718T204500Z
DTSTAMP:20260508T035733Z
